@node emacsctl Commands
@section emacsctl Commands

@subsection Global flags

Accepted anywhere on the command line:

@table @option
@item -c, --context NAME
use a named context from the config file
@item --instance SEL
target instance: @code{<pid>}, @code{primary}, or @code{auto}
@item --host DEST
remote editor over ssh (@code{user@@machine})
@item -o, --output FMT
@code{table} (default), @code{json}, @code{yaml}, @code{raw}
@item -w, --watch
stream changes where supported (@code{logs})
@item --timeout SECS
RPC timeout (default 30)
@item --config PATH
config file (default @file{~/.config/cmacs/emacsctl.yaml}, or
@env{EMACSCTL_CONFIG})
@item -q, --quiet
suppress non-essential output
@item --version
client version
@item -h, --help
contextual help (see below)
@end table

Precedence: command-line flags > the selected context > config
@code{settings:} > built-in defaults.

@subsection Getting help

Help is contextual and available everywhere (generated through
GLib's option parser, so flag listings can never drift from the
parser): @code{emacsctl --help} for the global overview,
@code{emacsctl GROUP --help} for a group's subcommands,
@code{emacsctl CMD --help} for one command's flags plus the global
options, and @code{emacsctl help WORDS@dots{}} as an equivalent
spelling.  A bare group prints its subcommand listing and exits 2;
an unknown flag exits 2 with a @option{--help} hint; a typo'd verb
gets a did-you-mean suggestion.

@subsection Core commands

@table @code
@item instances
list running cmacs instances (pid, version, primary, uptime)
@item describe instance
full identity JSON: pid, version, features[], start time, MCP socket
@item eval [--lang L] EXPR...
evaluate in the editor; L: @code{elisp} (default), @code{crispy},
@code{bacon}, @code{eshell}
@item repl [--lang L]
interactive REPL (@pxref{emacsctl REPL})
@item logs [-f] [-n N]
tail @file{*Messages*}; @option{-f} follows live via the
@code{MessageLogged} signal
@item watch stream
stream BufferAdded/Removed + FrameAdded/Removed events
@item config @dots{}
contexts (@pxref{emacsctl Contexts})
@item completion bash|zsh
shell completion script
@end table

@subsection Editor surface

Groups @code{get}, @code{buffer}, @code{edit}, @code{file},
@code{find-file}, @code{text} (whose verbs all take
@option{-b/--buffer NAME} and whose @code{insert}/@code{append} take @option{-e} to expand
backslash escapes like @command{echo -e} and read stdin when no
TEXT argument is given), @code{nav}, @code{search}, @code{vc},
@code{project}, @code{clip}, @code{bookmark}, @code{pkg},
@code{var}, @code{input}, @code{window}, @code{process},
@code{message}, @code{debug}, @code{c} (cintrospect/cpatch) and
@code{watch} cover the classic editing surface.  Run
@code{emacsctl help} for the live list with one-line summaries ---
the help output is generated from the same table that drives
dispatch, so it cannot go stale.

@subsection Subsystem groups

Available when the @emph{target} cmacs was built with the subsystem;
a missing subsystem maps to exit code 3 with a clear message:
@code{crispy}, @code{bacon}, @code{eshell}, @code{ai}, @code{gsurf},
@code{gnuseye}, @code{pod} (podomation), @code{video}, @code{audio},
@code{speech} (whisper + piper), @code{lrg} (libregnum editor),
@code{compositor} and @code{monitor} (gowl).

@subsection Org buffers: text insert-org

Org-aware insertion: target a headline (@option{-H} takes an exact
title at any depth, or a slash outline path like
@code{Projects/cmacs/Log}; @option{--create} makes missing
components), choose the spot inside the entry
(@option{-p top|bottom|subtree-end|point}; bottom --- the default ---
is the end of the entry's own body, before child headings), wrap the
text (@option{--quote}, @option{--src LANG}, @option{--example},
@option{--verse}, @option{--center}, @option{--block NAME},
@option{--drawer NAME}), or file it as a new child headline
(@option{--child TITLE} with optional @option{--todo} and
@option{--tags}).  @option{--timestamp} prepends an inactive org
timestamp; @option{-e} expands escapes; stdin feeds the text when no
argument is given.

@example
emacsctl text insert-org -b notes.org -H 'Projects/cmacs' \
  --src elisp '(message "hi")'
git log -1 | emacsctl text insert-org -b notes.org \
  -H 'Log/2026' --create --child "$(date +%F)" --todo TODO
@end example

Wrapping composes inside-out: block, then drawer, then the child
headline.  The underlying typed method is
@code{Editor1.Edit.InsertOrg} (options travel as an extensible
string dict), with MCP parity via the @code{insert_org} tool.

@subsection Org buffers: get content-org

@code{get content BUFFER} prints a buffer verbatim.  For org-mode
buffers, @code{get content-org} parses the document @emph{in the
editor} (org's own parser --- @code{org-map-entries}, planning info,
property drawers, the agenda match syntax) and returns a structured
tree, so @option{-o yaml} / @option{-o json} give you real data
instead of a text blob:

@example
emacsctl get content-org notes.org                # outline table
emacsctl get content-org notes.org -o yaml        # nested tree
emacsctl get content-org notes.org --match "/TODO" -o json
emacsctl get content-org notes.org --match "work+urgent" --depth 2
emacsctl get content-org notes.org --no-body --no-properties
@end example

@table @option
@item --match EXPR
org agenda match string: tags/todo/property queries like
@code{work+urgent/TODO} or @code{/DONE}
@item --depth N
limit headline depth
@item --no-body
structure only, drop entry body text
@item --no-properties
drop property drawers
@end table

Each headline carries @code{title}, @code{level}, @code{todo},
@code{priority}, @code{tags}, @code{scheduled} / @code{deadline} /
@code{closed}, @code{properties}, @code{body}, and @code{children};
the document carries @code{buffer}, @code{file}, and the
@code{#+TITLE} / @code{#+AUTHOR} / @code{#+DATE} / @code{#+FILETAGS}
keywords.  Keys are omitted when empty.  The default table output
renders an indented outline.

The underlying typed method is @code{Editor1.Edit.GetOrgContent},
with MCP parity via the @code{get_org_content} tool.

@subsection Output formats

@example
emacsctl get buffers                # aligned table (human)
emacsctl get buffers -o json        # pretty JSON array
emacsctl get buffers -o yaml        # YAML sequence
emacsctl get buffers -o raw         # one name per line, stable
emacsctl eval '(+ 1 2)' -o raw      # "3" --- byte-exact, no newline
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
# Save every modified buffer whose name ends in .org:
emacsctl get buffers -o raw \
  | grep '\.org$' \
  | while read -r b; do emacsctl buffer save "$b"; done

# Editor health one-liner for a status bar:
emacsctl describe instance -o json | jq -r .uptime

# Hot-patch a C primitive, then verify:
emacsctl c patch some-defun my_replacement_fn
emacsctl c patch-log
@end example
